        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Mumun culture</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">Southern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geum-river">Geum River</a> valleys of west-central Korea, the Nam River valley and the southern coast; key sites Daepyeong, Eoeun, Baekseok-dong, Deokcheon-ni, Igeum-dong and Namsan; its reach extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jeju-island">Jeju Island</a> and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1500–300 BCE: Early (Formative) Mumun c. 1500–850 BCE, Middle (Classic) Mumun c. 850–550 BCE, Late (Post-classic) Mumun 550–300 BCE; preceded by the Jeulmun pottery period (c. 8000–1500 BCE).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">plain, undecorated cooking and storage pottery; red-burnished pottery; polished groundstone daggers; megalithic burials; pit-houses and long-houses; dry-field and paddy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> farming beside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>; lunate stone harvesting knives; early bronze and greenstone prestige goods</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavated settlements, fields and burials, above all since a boom in excavation from the mid-1990s; the study of pottery, tools and houses; linguists’ reconstructions of the region’s early languages.</span></div>
