    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Mumun is an archaeologists’ label for Korean prehistory c. 1500–300 BCE, named after the Korean word for its plain, undecorated cooking and storage pottery.</li><li>Its farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millets</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, barley, wheat and legumes, and by the Middle Mumun worked large dry fields and paddy fields while still hunting and fishing.</li><li>Egalitarian villages gave way to large settlements, elite burials with bronze and jade and simple <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdom</a>-like polities, and finally to fortified hilltops in a time of conflict.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Early Mumun pit-houses could have as many as six hearths, which archaeologists read as homes shared by several generations of one household.</p></aside>
    </section>

